{
  "title": "Database Schema, Migrations, and Seeding",
  "overview": "The Xikolo project leverages PostgreSQL as its primary data store, serving both the main application and its suite of microservices. This comprehensive documentation outlines the architecture, configuration, schema design, migration strategies, and seeding processes that govern the project's data layer. Key components include the PostgreSQL Database itself, ActiveRecord for ORM, the Fx Gem for managing database functions, and the Scenic Gem for handling database views, all augmented by a rich set of PostgreSQL extensions.",
  "components": [
    "PostgreSQL Database",
    "ActiveRecord",
    "Fx Gem",
    "Scenic Gem",
    "PostgreSQL Extensions"
  ],
  "functional_domain": "Database Management",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Database Configuration",
      "description": "Database configurations are centrally managed through `config/database.yml`, ensuring consistency across the main application and all microservices (e.g., account, course, news, notification, pinboard, quiz, timeeffort). All environments (development, test, integration, production) share common settings, including the `postgresql` adapter, a configurable connection pool size, and a statement timeout of 10 seconds. These settings are largely driven by environment variables such as `DATABASE_URL`, `CONCURRENCY`, and `RAILS_MAX_THREADS`, promoting flexible deployment.",
      "sources": [
        "config/database.yml",
        "services/account/config/database.yml",
        "services/course/config/database.yml",
        "services/news/config/database.yml",
        "services/notification/config/database.yml",
        "services/pinboard/config/database.yml",
        "services/quiz/config/database.yml",
        "services/timeeffort/config/database.yml"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Schema Design and Features",
      "description": "The database schema, defined in `db/schema.rb` and auto-generated by Active Record (currently based on `ActiveRecord::Schema[7.2]`), reflects a robust learning platform. A key architectural decision is the extensive utilization of PostgreSQL-specific features, managed in part by the Fx and Scenic gems.",
      "sub_sections": [
        {
          "title": "PostgreSQL Extensions",
          "details": "The schema explicitly enables `hstore` (for key-value storage), `pg_trgm` (for trigram-based full-text search), `pgcrypto` (for cryptographic functions), `plpgsql` (for procedural language support), `unaccent` (for accent removal), and `uuid-ossp` (for UUID generation).",
          "sources": [
            "db/schema.rb"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Custom UUID Generation",
          "details": "Most tables use UUIDs as primary keys, with a custom PostgreSQL function `uuid_generate_v7ms()` serving as the default generator. This function, managed via the Fx Gem, generates UUID v7 with millisecond precision, indicating a modern approach to unique identifier generation.",
          "sources": [
            "db/schema.rb",
            "Source 1 (C04_Database_Schema_Migrations)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Enums",
          "details": "Several custom enums are defined to constrain column values, including `link_target`, `node_type`, `offer_category`, `payment_frequency`, and `sort_mode`.",
          "sources": [
            "db/schema.rb"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Advanced Data Types",
          "details": "The schema makes heavy use of `jsonb` for storing structured JSON data (e.g., `translations`, `recipients`, `config`, `vendor_data`) and `hstore` for key-value pairs (e.g., `description`, `payload`, `policy_url`, `preferences`, `settings`). Array columns are also prevalent for storing lists of values (e.g., `groups`, `teacher_ids`, `learning_goals`, `tags`, `permissions`).",
          "sources": [
            "db/schema.rb"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Full-Text Search",
          "details": "The `questions` table includes a `tsv` column of type `tsvector` with a `gin_trgm_ops` opclass index, indicating advanced full-text search capabilities utilizing `pg_trgm`.",
          "sources": [
            "db/schema.rb"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Views",
          "details": "A materialized view `embed_courses` is defined, which denormalizes course data by joining with `classifiers` and `teachers` to provide a more efficient query interface for certain course attributes. This view is managed using the Scenic Gem.",
          "sources": [
            "db/schema.rb",
            "Source 1 (C04_Database_Schema_Migrations)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Foreign Keys",
          "details": "Explicit foreign key constraints are defined between various tables, ensuring data integrity and maintaining relationships across the schema.",
          "sources": [
            "db/schema.rb"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        "db/schema.rb",
        "Source 1 (C04_Database_Schema_Migrations)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Database Migrations",
      "description": "Database migrations are a critical part of the project's development workflow. The `docs/app/development/best_practices/database_migrations.md` document outlines best practices, emphasizing that migrations are used for both schema and data alterations and should ideally be reversible.",
      "sub_sections": [
        {
          "title": "Data Migration Workflow",
          "details": "For data migrations, a multi-step workflow is recommended: creating new tables, adapting code to write to both old and new resources, migrating data, updating code to use only the new resource, and finally removing deprecated attributes.",
          "sources": [
            "docs/app/development/best_practices/database_migrations.md"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Performance Considerations",
          "details": "Performance considerations for migrations include using bulk table migrations, batch processing (`find_each`, `in_batches`), dropping and re-adding indices, and judiciously skipping validations or using plain SQL for efficiency.",
          "sources": [
            "docs/app/development/best_practices/database_migrations.md"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Best Practices",
          "details": "It is also recommended to mock models within migrations to prevent future breakage due to model name changes.",
          "sources": [
            "docs/app/development/best_practices/database_migrations.md"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        "docs/app/development/best_practices/database_migrations.md"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Seed Data",
      "description": "Seed data is loaded via `db/seeds.rb`, which dynamically loads all Ruby files from `db/seeds/all/**/*.rb` and then `db/seeds/<env>/**/*.rb` (where `<env>` defaults to `Rails.env` or can be overridden by `SEED_ENV`). This allows for a structured and environment-specific approach to populating initial database content, with alphabetical ordering ensuring consistent load order.",
      "sources": [
        "db/seeds.rb"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
